WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s crazy when you think about all of
the different kinds of foods we eat.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.760
We just swallow and hope
it all works out for the best.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Well, as it turns out,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
there are better ways to think about keeping
our bodies humming healthfully along.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.040
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:21.840
In this episode,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.720
we look at ways to change our diet
for the good of the whole planet.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.440
In our first story,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.320
the EAT-Lancet Commission
lays out the best diet

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.520
for human and planetary health.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.480
Scientists have a clear moral
obligation to clearly warn humanity

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.640
of any catastrophic threat
and to tell it like it is.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.760
In November 2019, more than
11,000 scientists from 150 countries

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.040
clearly and unequivocally
declared that planet Earth

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.800
is facing a climate emergency.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.400
CO2 levels are rising, the glaciers
are melting, Antarctica is melting.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.760
The oceans are getting
hotter, more acidic.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.480
Sea levels are rising, and so
are extreme weather events.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.400
And yes, fossil fuel use is
going up, like air travel, but

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.640
so is per capita meat consumption.
In fact, one of the solutions

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.000
they offer to help the climate
crisis is eating mostly

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.920
plant-based foods while reducing the
global consumption of animal products.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.120
And what makes designing a sustainable
diet so easy is that the same advice—

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.120
like eat less meat—is good for
both personal health, like reducing

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.920
the risk of our number #1 killer,
as well as for planetary health.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.160
The least healthy foods also cause
the worst environmental impact.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.480
The foods with the most nutrition
just so happen to be the foods

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.880
that cause the lowest greenhouse gas
emissions, so you get this win-win effect.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.360
So let's put it all together.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.000
If we are to redesign the global food
system for human and planetary health—

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.880
which is to say human health and future
human health—what would it look like?

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.400
Enter: the EAT-Lancet Commission,
the result of more than two years

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.320
of collaboration between 37 experts
from 16 countries, suggesting

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.480
a cut in total meat consumption
down to like an ounce a day—

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.960
that's like the weight of
a single chicken nugget—

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.360
all the while dramatically
increasing our intakes

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.320
of legumes—which are beans,
split peas, chickpeas, and lentils—

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.880
nuts, fruits, and vegetables,

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.040
because we're not just in a
climate crisis, but a health crisis.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.480
Unhealthy diets cause more death
and disease than smoking,

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.360
more than unsafe sex, and alcohol,
drug, and tobacco use combined.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.960
But we can address both
crises at the same time

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.680
by increasing our consumption
of whole plant foods

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.000
and substantially reducing our
consumption of animal source foods.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.520
Eating such a diet could save the lives
of more than 10 million people a year

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.800
and may just help save the world.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.160
The Paris Agreement had set
out a boundary condition,

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.000
an aspirational goal for a carbon budget
to help prevent catastrophic impacts,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.320
and staying within the
boundary for climate change

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.080
can be achieved by consuming
plant-based diets.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.880
And the personal benefits may be
comparable with or even exceed

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.520
the value of the environmental benefits.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.800
The healthcare benefits alone
for a healthy global diet—

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.600
a predominantly plant-based diet,
a vegetarian or a vegan diet—

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.040
could exceed the price
of the carbon saved.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.040
We're talking up to $30
trillion dollars a year saved

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.960
from the health benefits alone.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.960
Now if the health of yourself,
the planet, and your own children

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.800
doesn't quite motivate you, consider
that you may also be facing threats

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.800
to the global beer supply.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.240
And healthier diets don't just
reduce greenhouse gas emissions.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.720
Since livestock production is the
single largest driver of habitat loss,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.560
reducing meat consumption
is also the key

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.520
to biodiversity conservation,
ideally, perhaps, reducing demand

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.440
for animal-based
foods by increasing

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.000
the proportion of plant-based
foods up to like 90% of the diet.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.440
Livestock production is also a
leading cause of soil loss, water

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.400
and nutrient pollution. Yet it appears
to be a blind spot in water policy.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.400
Despite the fact that animal products
form the single most important factor

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.560
in humanity's water footprint,

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.400
water managers never seem
to talk about meat and dairy.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.000
But it's not just animal products.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.400
I mean, yes, at least 80% of
the deforestation in the Amazon

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.360
is to raise cattle and grow
feed crops like soybeans

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.120
to export to other farm animals,

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.160
but also to make vegetable oil,
most of which is from palm and soy.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.040
Both crops have been expanding,
resulting in massive deforestation.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.240
It just seems particularly egregious
if that deforestation takes place

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.720
for the sake of junk food.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.520
Not everyone agrees we should
be moving to healthier diets, though.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.720
The World Health Organization actually
pulled out of the EAT-Lancet Commission

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.040
because of their promotion of a global
move to more plant-based foods.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.560
See, if we focused on promoting
predominantly plant-based foods

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.000
and excluding foods deemed
unhealthy, including meat and other

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.120
animal-based foods, such a diet could,
yeah, save 10 million lives a year,

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.360
$30 trillion dollars, and help save
the entire planet, but could lead

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.520
to the loss of jobs linked to animal
husbandry and the production of junk.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:43.600
In our next story,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.840
we look at the purported
decline of nutrients in our crops

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.480
due to soil degradation,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.880
or is that just supplement
industry propaganda?

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.800
Internet articles
and the popular press

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:55.680
frequently state that

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.160
the mineral nutrient
composition of crops

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.560
has been declining
over the last 50 years

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.520
with titles like
"The great nutrient collapse,"

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.520
blamed in part on the purported
nutrient depletion of the soil.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.600
This narrative is touted
by the leading

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.480
dietary supplement
trade association,

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.320
but is it true?

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.160
This is probably
the most cited study,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.640
comparing the nutrient content
of U.S. fruits and vegetables

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.280
between 1950 and 1999,

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.480
and about half,

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.640
seven of the 13
nutrients tested,

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.960
showed no significant decline,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.320
and the six that did

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.600
only declined by about
15 percent on average.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.400
A similar study in the UK
looking at mineral content

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.200
had similar findings
with declines in about half.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.040
Of course, the drop in sodium
doesn't matter;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.720
we get too much of it as it is,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.040
and most people
get more than enough copper.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.480
But the ten percent drop
in magnesium is significant,

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.120
and though the drop in iron
looks precipitous,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.640
the absolute drop is tiny,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.920
since fruits and veggies
have so little to begin with.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.240
Australian researchers
had similar findings:

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.960
a halving of iron in fruit
over the last few decades,

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.160
but the absolute drop
is like 0.2 mg per serving,

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.600
which is about 1/50th

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.000
of the recommended
daily allowance for most people,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.520
and the same with zinc.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.904
And there was actually
an increase in grains.

00:07:09.904 --> 00:07:12.080
As you can see, there was just
a few milligrams difference

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.680
in calcium or magnesium,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.320
where the RDA is in
the hundreds of milligrams.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.040
So, with such small
absolute differences,

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.040
any true decrease
in mineral content

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.720
is unlikely
to have a large impact

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.160
on nutritional intake.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.240
And it's not clear
if these changes are even real.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.880
These comparisons of historical
food composition tables

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.520
are not a reliable way
to determine changes

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.920
in nutrient composition
of foods over time,

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.200
since they are just
unmatched snapshots in time

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.480
not meant to sync up.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.120
So, these may have been
different varieties,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.720
or there may have been
differences

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.360
in ripeness, season, location.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.880
If you look at
the levels of minerals

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:50.240
in the soil itself,

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.640
you don't tend
to see declining levels,

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:54.000
and in some cases,
you actually see

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.920
a significant rise
in mineral levels

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.880
nearly across the board
over the last half century.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:00.720
So, where is this gloomy

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.920
supplement industry propaganda
coming from?

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.160
There's been a concern
that rising CO2 levels

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.160
might reduce nutrients in crops,

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.840
but the largest recorded change

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.960
was a 59 percent increase
in total antioxidant content,

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.840
thanks to higher
phytonutrient levels.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.000
What about the concern
that modern varieties

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.000
grow so much larger and faster,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.520
potentially creating a trade-off

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.200
between yield
and nutrient concentration?

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.120
One way to study this
is to grow a whole bunch

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.120
of different varieties
released over the years

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.120
under the same conditions,

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.520
which is what these researchers
did with broccoli.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.400
14 cultivars
released over 50 years,

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.800
and they found that there was
no clear change over time

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:39.920
in mineral content,

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.480
though they did find
that larger heads of broccoli

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.400
did tend to have
lower mineral content;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.040
something to keep in mind
next time you shop.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.320
As for the dietary supplement
trade association's claim

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.520
that Americans don't get the
nutrients they need from foods?

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.080
Well, for example,
only half of Americans

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.360
reach even 75 mg
of vitamin C a day--

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.320
less than the amount
in a single orange.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.840
So, why aren't Americans

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.320
getting enough fruit
and vegetable nutrients?

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.800
Because they aren't eating
enough fruits and vegetables.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.840
They don't need pills,
they just need produce.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:13.360
So, even if there really is

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.560
15 percent less vitamin C
in today's broccoli,

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.240
that just may mean
you have to eat

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.080
six florets of today's broccoli

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.320
instead of five florets
from yesteryear.

